2| * hermes-turn-hook.ts — Hermes's native per-turn hook for the brain-first
3| * rule: a marked `hooks.pre_llm_call` shell hook in $HERMES_HOME/config.yaml
4| * that echoes `{"context": <rule>}`, which Hermes appends to every user
5| * message. The block in SOUL.md reaches the system prompt only, and bots read
6| * it as identity; this puts the same rule next to each turn. The command is
7| * self-contained (no gbrain process per turn, no path that must exist on the
8| * Hermes host), so a re-run after an upgrade rewrites it with the new text.
9| *
10| * Ownership is by YAML comment markers; text outside them is never touched.
11| * gbrain appends its block only when config.yaml has no top-level `hooks:`
12| * key (a second one would be invalid YAML) and prints the entry to add by
13| * hand otherwise. Hermes runs a shell hook only after approval, so setup
14| * records the approval for this exact command in Hermes's own allowlist, the
15| * way `gbrain setup codex` writes its hook trust entries. Removal strips the
16| * block and that approval.
17| *
18| * The same block also carries the skill-resolver lane's entry
19| * (`gbrain setup hermes --skill-loading resolve`, skill-resolver-hook.ts):
20| * config.yaml can hold one top-level `hooks:` key, so both lanes share one
21| * marked block, each entry with its own approval. Each lane writes and removes
22| * only its own entry, so declining the brain-first rule keeps the resolver
23| * and the reverse; the block goes when its last entry does.
24| */
